    Stats  [label="Statistics", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    Data -> Signal -> Pos -> Fills -> PnL -> Stats;
}
```

## Simulating a Strategy

* From Signals to Positions
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:110 '* Backtesting: definition'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1958 '* Walk-forward method'

- @Definition@: a backtest maps a **signal** $s_{i,t}$ for asset $i$ to a
  **target weight** $w_{i,t}$, then charges the return that follows
  $$
  PnL_{t+1}
    \triangleq \sum_i w_{i,t} \, r_{i,t+1}
      - c \sum_i |w_{i,t} - w_{i,t-1}|
  $$
  - $c$ is the cost per unit traded, and $\sum_i |w_{i,t} - w_{i,t-1}|$ is
    the turnover

- @Assumptions@: $w_{i,t}$ uses only the information available at the
  decision time $t$
  - The return $r_{i,t+1}$ comes strictly after the decision

- @Example@: the momentum book at the end of month $t$
  - $s_{i,t}$ is the 12-1 month return, and the weights are $\pm 1/n$ on the
    top and bottom deciles
  - The book is dollar neutral: $\sum_i w_{i,t} = 0$, with gross exposure
    $\sum_i |w_{i,t}| = 2$

- @Key idea@: one wrong time index, such as $r_{i,t}$ instead of
  $r_{i,t+1}$, turns a backtest into a forecast of the past

* Fills and Execution Assumptions
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:124 '* Profitability of strategy depends on details of implementation'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:316 '* Primary vs consolidated stock prices'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1863 '* Transaction cost effects'

- @Problem@: the PnL depends on details of execution that a simple backtest
  ignores
  - Market-on-open order or market order just after the open?
  - Stock close at 4:00 pm or futures close at 4:15 pm?
  - Trigger on the last trade price or on the bid and ask?

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Fill model", "Assumption", "Typical error"),
  rows: (
    ("Signal-bar price", "Trade at the price that produced the signal", "Look-ahead and bid-ask bounce"),
    ("Next-bar price", "Trade one bar after the signal", "Ignores the spread"),
    ("Mid plus half spread", "Pay half the spread to cross", "Ignores impact of large orders"),
    ("Impact model", "Cost grows with participation rate", "Needs calibration on own fills"),
    ("Limit order with queue", "Fill only when the price trades through", "Optimistic if the queue is ignored"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1fr, 1.5fr, 1.3fr),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Remark@: consolidated open and close prices, highs, and lows may come from
  venues where the order could not have executed

* Costs Inside the Backtest
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:333 '* Backtest needs to incorporate costs'
// From: book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/lectures_source/Lesson01.01_Why_Finance_Is_Different.smd:554 '* Transaction Costs'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Fact@: net return is gross return minus turnover times cost per unit
  traded, so the **breakeven cost** is
  $$
  c^* \triangleq \frac{\mu_{gross}}{turnover}
  $$

- @Example@: an 8% gross edge breaks even at
  - 67 bps when rebalanced monthly (12x)
  - 15 bps weekly (52x), 3 bps daily (252x)

- @Key idea@: a high Sharpe ratio before costs can turn negative after costs,
  so faster strategies need more precise cost models
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

![](book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/lectures_source/figures/L02.01.Cost_Sensitivity.png)

\footnotesize _Net Sharpe ratio against cost per unit traded for the same gross
edge at three rebalancing frequencies_
::::
:::

* Vectorized vs. Event-Driven Backtesters
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:626 '* Backtesting vs execution platforms'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:135 '* Backtesting and automated execution'

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Aspect", "Vectorized", "Event-driven"),
  rows: (
    ("Core idea", "Array operations on the full panel of data", "Loop over bars, quotes, and fills in time order"),
    ("Speed", "Fast: sweeps over many variants", "Slower: one pass per simulation"),
    ("Look-ahead risk", "High: one wrong shift leaks the future", "Low: only past events are visible"),
    ("Orders and fills", "Approximated by a fill price", "Modeled: queues, partial fills, latency"),
    ("Live trading", "Separate code to maintain", "Same code switches to live trading"),
  ),
  col-widths: (0.9fr, 1.4fr, 1.4fr),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Key idea@: screen ideas with a vectorized backtester, and validate the
  survivors with an event-driven one

- @Remark@: the backtester should become the execution system by flipping a
  switch
  - One code path avoids, or at least detects, look-ahead bias
  - It removes the drift between the backtested and the traded strategy

* Backtest Statistics to Report
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:2108 '* Time range'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:2147 '* Annualized turnover'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:2150 '* Correlation to underlying'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:2217 '* Return on execution costs'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:2272 '* Sharpe Ratio as function of hit ratio'

- @Problem@: a single return number hides how the PnL was produced

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Group", "Statistics", "What it reveals"),
  rows: (
    ("General", "Time range, leverage, capacity, ratio of longs", "Regimes covered, borrowing, scalability, hidden bias"),
    ("Bets", "Frequency of bets, holding period, turnover", "Number of independent bets and cost exposure"),
    ("Exposure", "Correlation to the underlying universe", "Beta sold as alpha"),
    ("Performance", "PnL by long and short side, hit ratio", "Which side and which trades earn the PnL"),
    ("Risk", "Maximum drawdown, time under water", "Pain an investor must tolerate"),
    ("Costs", "Return on execution costs", "Margin of safety against worse execution"),
  ),
  col-widths: (0.8fr, 1.5fr, 1.5fr),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Example@: a strategy with $n$ independent bets per year and hit ratio $p$
  on symmetric payoffs has
  $SR = \frac{2p - 1}{2 \sqrt{p (1 - p)}} \sqrt{n}$
  - $p = 52\%$ with $n = 252$ bets gives $SR \approx 0.64$

## Look-Ahead Bias

* Look-Ahead Bias
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:172 '* Look-ahead bias'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:287 '* Look-ahead bias'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1845 '* Look-ahead bias'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: **look-ahead bias** is the use of information that was not
  available at the time of the simulated decision

- @Example@: _"buy within 1% of the day's low"_ uses a low known only at the
  close

- @Example@: a trend rule on a random walk
  - Trading the next day gives $SR = 0.0$
  - Earning the signal day's return gives $SR = 5.2$

- @Key idea@: a daily $SR > 3$ is a bug until proven otherwise
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

![](book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/lectures_source/figures/L02.01.Look_Ahead_Bias.png)

\footnotesize _Cumulative PnL of a moving-average rule on a random walk, traded
the next day (blue) and on the signal day (red)_
::::
:::

* Subtle Sources of Leakage
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1845 '* Look-ahead bias'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson02.6-ML_Techniques_How_To_Do_Research.smd:125 '* Data Snooping (2/2)'

- @Problem@: most look-ahead bugs do not look like a shifted index

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Source", "Example", "Fix"),
  rows: (
    ("Release dates", "Earnings dated by fiscal quarter end, released weeks later", "Use the time the data became public"),
    ("Revisions", "Database stores restated fundamentals or revised GDP", "Use values as first reported"),
    ("Backfill", "Vendor adds history when a fund or stock enters coverage", "Use data as of its collection date"),
    ("Universe", "Today's index constituents used for the past", "Use historical membership"),
    ("Preprocessing", "Z-score features with the full-sample mean and variance", "Fit transforms on the training window only"),
    ("Model selection", "Hyperparameters tuned on all the data", "Select inside each training window"),
  ),
  col-widths: (0.8fr, 1.7fr, 1.4fr),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Key idea@: every input needs a knowledge timestamp, and the backtest must
  check it against the decision time

- @Remark@: point-in-time data engineering is covered in detail in Lesson
  04.01

## Survivorship Bias

* Survivorship Bias
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:266 '* Survivorship bias: example'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:288 '* Survivorship bias for mean-reverting strategy'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:306 '* Survivorship bias for momentum strategy'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:130 '* Does data suffer from survivorship bias?'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: **survivorship bias** occurs when the data omit assets that
  went bankrupt, merged, or delisted

- @Example@: buy last month's worst stocks
  - A survivor-only database keeps only the losers that recovered
  - 4.1% per year on all stocks, 11.8% on survivors

- @Characteristics@: the sign depends on the strategy
  - _Long losers_: inflated
  - _Short losers_: deflated
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

![](book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/lectures_source/figures/L02.01.Survivorship_Bias.png)

\footnotesize _Buy-the-losers strategy on a simulated universe in which 108 of
500 stocks delist_
::::
:::

