// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1856 '* Storytelling'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1868 '* Betting on outliers'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1879 '* Other common errors in backtesting'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:147 '* How did the performance of the strategy change over the years?'

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Pitfall", "What goes wrong"),
  rows: (
    ("Short-sale constraints", "Hard-to-borrow stocks, uptick rules, and short bans (e.g., 2008) block simulated shorts"),
    ("Financing", "Borrowing costs, margin calls, and stop-losses are left out"),
    ("Futures rolls", "Back-adjusted continuous contracts create prices that never traded"),
    ("Betting on outliers", "A few extreme events produce most of the PnL"),
    ("Storytelling", "A story is invented after the fact to explain a random pattern"),
    ("Unrepresentative period", "The sample covers one regime, or an older era with wider spreads"),
  ),
  col-widths: (1fr, 2.6fr),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Remark@: storytelling is also called HARKing (hypothesizing after the
  results are known)

- @Key idea@: each pitfall makes the simulated world friendlier than the market

## Why AI

* Automating Backtest Correctness
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:484 '* Testing by paper trading'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:501 '* Why does actual performance diverge from expectation'

- @Problem@: unrealistic fills and leaked data are software bugs, not
  statistical noise
  - They inflate the backtest silently, and no statistic flags them

- @Solution@: treat the backtester as tested production code
  - _Event-driven engine_: one code path for the backtest and live trading
  - _Unit-tested fill and cost models_: known orders produce known fills and
    known costs
  - _As-of assertions_: every data access checks its knowledge time against
    the decision time
  - _Generated tests_: perturb data after time $t$ and assert that positions
    up to $t$ do not change

- @Applications@: coding assistants generate as-of tests, perturbation tests,
  and fill-model tests from a written specification of the strategy

- @Remark@: paper trading is the final check
  - The gap between paper and backtest PnL should come only from costs

# Backtest Discipline

## Overfitting and Data Snooping

* Two Types of Overfitting
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Machine_learning_for_asset_managers.de_Prado.2020.md:171 '* Effect of Overfitting'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Machine_learning_for_asset_managers.de_Prado.2020.md:176 '* Train set overfitting'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Machine_learning_for_asset_managers.de_Prado.2020.md:190 '* Test set overfitting'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Machine_learning_for_asset_managers.de_Prado.2020.md:198 '* Test set overfitting: possible solutions'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson02.6-ML_Techniques_How_To_Do_Research.smd:137 '* "Burning the Test Set"'

- @Comparison@:

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("", "Train-set overfitting", "Test-set overfitting"),
  rows: (
    ("Cause", "A model flexible enough to fit signal and noise", "Many tests on the same test data"),
    ("Finance form", "Too many parameters for few independent bets", "Backtest, tweak, backtest again"),
    ("Symptom", "In-sample fit far above out-of-sample fit", "A selected backtest far above its live result"),
    ("Remedies", "Cross-validation, regularization, ensembles", "Count trials, use many test sets, simulate data"),
  ),
  col-widths: (0.7fr, 1.5fr, 1.5fr),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Key idea@: standard ML practice guards against train-set overfitting, but
  backtest research mostly suffers from test-set overfitting

// TODO(gp): Check the source and year of the Coase quote: the msml610 slide
// cites it as (Coase, 1982) without a reference
- @Remark@: _"If you torture the data long enough, it will confess whatever
  you want"_ (Coase)

* Backtest Overfitting
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1912 '* Backtest overfitting'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1909 '* Selection bias'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:186 '* Research haircut'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:190 '* Data-snooping bias'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: **backtest overfitting** selects a strategy that monetizes
  random historical patterns, which are unlikely to recur

- @Example@: 200 strategies with no skill, 2 years in-sample and 2 years
  out-of-sample
  - The best in-sample Sharpe ratio is 2.14
  - Its out-of-sample Sharpe ratio is 0.04

- @Definition@: **selection bias** reports only the backtests that look like
  winners

- @Fact@: a research haircut of 50% on the Sharpe ratio from in-sample to
  out-of-sample is considered a good result
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

![](book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/lectures_source/figures/L02.01.IS_vs_OOS_Sharpe.png)

\footnotesize _In-sample against out-of-sample Sharpe ratio of 200 zero-skill
strategies, with the in-sample winner in red_
::::
:::

* The False Strategy Theorem
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Machine_learning_for_asset_managers.de_Prado.2020.md:700 '# 8, Testing set overfitting'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Machine_learning_for_asset_managers.de_Prado.2020.md:704 '# B, Proof of the false strategy theorem'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Fact@: the expected maximum Sharpe ratio of $N$ independent zero-skill
  trials is
  $$
  \EE\left[\max_n \widehat{SR}_n\right]
    \approx \sqrt{\VV[\widehat{SR}_n]} \, z_N
  $$
  $$
  z_N \triangleq
    (1 - \gamma) Z^{-1}\left[1 - \frac{1}{N}\right]
    + \gamma Z^{-1}\left[1 - \frac{1}{N e}\right]
  $$
  - $Z^{-1}$ is the inverse standard Gaussian CDF and $\gamma \approx 0.5772$
    is the Euler-Mascheroni constant

- @Example@: 5-year backtests have $\sqrt{\VV[\widehat{SR}_n]} \approx 0.45$,
  so the expected best $SR$ is 1.13 for 100 trials and 1.46 for 1,000

- @Key idea@: the Sharpe ratio hurdle must rise with the number of trials
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

![](book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/lectures_source/figures/L02.01.Expected_Max_Sharpe.png)

\footnotesize _Expected maximum Sharpe ratio against the number of zero-skill
trials: closed form (blue) and Monte Carlo (red)_
::::
:::

## Multiple Testing and False Discoveries

* Multiple Testing in Finance
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:650 '## 2019, Harvey, An Evaluation of Alternative Multiple Testing Methods for Finance Applications'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:464 '## 2018, de Prado, A practical solution to the multiple-testing crisis in financial research'
// From: book_cs_refreshers/lectures_source/Lesson91.Refresher_probability.smd:2857 '* Example of data mining (jelly beans and acne)'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1902 '* Multiple testing'

- @Question@: a manager beats the market 10 years in a row, with
  $p \approx 0.001$ under no skill; is the manager skilled?
  - Among 10,000 unskilled managers, about 10 do the same by luck

- @Definition@: the **family-wise error rate** (FWER) is the probability of
  at least one false discovery among $m$ tests at level $\alpha$
  $$
  FWER \triangleq \Pr(V \ge 1) = 1 - (1 - \alpha)^m
  $$
  - The formula holds for $m$ independent tests of true null hypotheses
  - E.g., $m = 20$ tests at $\alpha = 5\%$ give $FWER \approx 64\%$

- @Problem@: papers and pitch books report the final p-value, not the number
  of trials behind it
  - Any Sharpe ratio is achievable on a false theory with enough trials

- @Key idea@: a p-value is meaningful only together with the number of tests
  that produced it

* Controlling FWER: Bonferroni and Holm
// From: book_cs_refreshers/lectures_source/Lesson91.Refresher_probability.smd:2973 '* Bonferroni correction to control FWER'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:792 '* Controlling FWER'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:807 '* Bonferroni method'

- @Definition@: **Bonferroni** declares test $i$ significant when
  $$
  p_i \le \frac{\alpha}{m}
  $$
  - It guarantees $FWER \le \alpha$ for any dependence between tests

- @Example@: $m = 100$ strategies at $\alpha = 5\%$ need $p \le 0.0005$,
  i.e., a one-sided $t \ge 3.29$
  - With $m = 1000$, the hurdle rises to $t \ge 3.89$

- @Definition@: **Holm** sorts the p-values $p_{(1)} \le \dots \le p_{(m)}$
  and rejects in order while
  $$
  p_{(i)} \le \frac{\alpha}{m - i + 1}
  $$
  - It stops at the first failure

- @Pros@: both control FWER, and Holm rejects at least as much as Bonferroni

- @Cons@: in finance, where most strategies are near the noise level, FWER
  control is so strict that it misses most true signals

* Controlling FDR: Benjamini-Hochberg
// From: book_cs_refreshers/lectures_source/Lesson91.Refresher_probability.smd:3011 '* False Discovery Rate'
// From: book_cs_refreshers/lectures_source/Lesson91.Refresher_probability.smd:3043 '* BH to control FDR'
// From: book_cs_refreshers/lectures_source/Lesson91.Refresher_probability.smd:3064 '* BY to control FDR'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:841 '#### Controlling FDR'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: the **false discovery rate** is the expected fraction of
  false discoveries $V$ among the discoveries $R$
  $$
  FDR \triangleq \EE\left[\frac{V}{R}\right]
  $$

- @Definition@: **Benjamini-Hochberg** (BH) finds the largest $k$ with
  $p_{(k)} \le \alpha k / m$ and rejects the $k$ smallest

- @Example@: 100 strategies, 10 with skill, $\alpha = 5\%$
  - _No correction_: 14 picks, 4 false
  - _Bonferroni_: 4 picks, all true
  - _BH_: 9 picks, 1 false

- @Remark@: BH assumes independent or positively dependent tests
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

![](book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/lectures_source/figures/L02.01.BH_Procedure.png)

\footnotesize _Sorted p-values of 100 strategies against the uncorrected,
Bonferroni, and BH thresholds_
::::
:::

* The t > 3 Hurdle
// From: book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/paper_list.md:138 '### [ ] 2016, Harvey et al., "...and the Cross-Section of Expected Returns"'
// From: book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/paper_list.md:129 '### [ ] 2020, Hou et al., "Replicating Anomalies"'
// From: book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/paper_list.md:111 '### [ ] 2023, Jensen et al., "Is There a Replication Crisis in Finance?"'

- @Problem@: hundreds of published factors claim to explain the cross-section
  of expected returns
  - With this much data mining, the usual $t > 2$ is too weak

- @Fact@: **Harvey, Liu, and Zhu** (2016) model correlated tests and
  publication bias, and estimate that a new factor needs $t > 3.0$

- @Fact@: **Hou, Xue, and Zhang** (2020) replicate 452 anomalies
  - 65% fail the single-test hurdle $|t| \ge 1.96$
  - 82% fail the multiple-test hurdle $|t| \ge 2.78$

- @Comparison@: **Jensen, Kelly, and Pedersen** (2023) find that most factors
  replicate
  - With a Bayesian model, factors cluster into 13 themes and work out of
    sample across 93 countries

- @Key idea@: whether a factor is real depends on the number of factors tried,
  the dependence between them, and the prior plausibility of each

## Cross-Validation for Finance

* Why K-Fold CV Fails in Finance
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1498 '* Why CV fails in finance?'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1505 '* Leakage'
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson05.3-Learn_Validation.smd:173 '* Cross-validation: Algorithm'

- @Definition@: **k-fold cross-validation** splits the data into $k$ folds,
  trains on $k - 1$ of them, and tests on the remaining one
  - It assumes that observations are IID

- @Problem@: financial observations break this assumption
  1. _Overlapping labels_: a label at $t$ uses returns from $t + 1$ to
     $t + h$, so neighbors share outcomes
  2. _Serial correlation_: features close in time carry the same information
  3. _Reuse_: the same data is used many times while developing a model

- @Example@: monthly observations labeled with the next 3-month return
  - Consecutive labels share 2 of 3 months
  - A training row next to the test fold already knows most of the test
    label

- @Key idea@: **leakage** is training data that contains information about
  the test data, and it inflates CV scores as look-ahead inflates backtests

* Walk-Forward Validation
// From: msml610/lectures_source/Lesson10.1-Timeseries_forecasting.smd:1193 '* Cross-Validation for Time Series'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1963 '* Walk-forward method: cons'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:310 '* Out-of-sample testing'

- @Definition@: **walk-forward validation** trains on the past and tests on the
  next period, then rolls the window forward
  - _Rolling window_: fixed length, forgets old regimes
  - _Expanding window_: keeps all history, adapts slowly

- @Pros@:
  - Mimics live trading: every decision uses only earlier data
  - Gives a performance estimate for each period

- @Cons@:
  - Tests a single path, which can be retried until a false positive appears
  - The past sequence of regimes is one draw (e.g., 2007, 2008, and 2012 are
    very different markets)
  - Early decisions use little data, and a warm-up period shortens the test
  - Mixes generalization error with non-stationarity

- @Key idea@: walk-forward is the most realistic single test, and the easiest
  one to overfit by repetition

* Purging Overlapping Labels
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1511 '* Purging the training set'

- @Problem@: the leakage comes from the labels $Y$, not from the features $X$

- @Definition@: **purging** removes from the training set every observation
  whose label window overlaps the label window of any test observation
  - Observation $i$ has label window $[t_{i,0}, t_{i,1}]$
  - Training observation $i$ is purged if, for some test observation $j$
    $$
    [t_{i,0}, t_{i,1}] \cap [t_{j,0}, t_{j,1}] \neq \emptyset
    $$

- @Example@: daily rows labeled with the 5-day forward return
  - Purge the 5 training rows before the test block, whose labels end inside
    it
  - Purge the training rows after the test block whose labels overlap the
    last test labels

- @Key idea@: purging costs a few training rows per fold and removes the
  shared outcomes between train and test

* Embargo
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1518 '* Embargo process'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1523 '* Purging / embargo of cross-validation'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=45%}
- @Problem@: serially correlated features leak information from the test
  block into the training rows right after it

- @Definition@: an **embargo** drops $h_e$ further observations after each
  test block, beyond the purged ones
// TODO(gp): Check that de Prado (2018, ch. 7) suggests h_e = 0.01T: it is
// recalled from memory and is not in the notes
  - A small value such as $h_e \approx 0.01 T$ often suffices

- @Remark@: leave-one-out CV leaks the most, since every test point borders
  the training set
::::
:::: {.column width=55%}

![](book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/lectures_source/figures/L02.01.Purged_KFold.png)

\footnotesize _Purged and embargoed 5-fold CV with 3-step labels and a 2-step
embargo_
::::
:::

* Combinatorial Purged CV
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:2069 '* Combinatorial purged CV method'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:2064 '* Backtesting through cross-validation'

- @Problem@: one walk-forward path yields one Sharpe ratio, which is easy to
  overfit

- @Definition@: **combinatorial purged CV** (CPCV) splits the data into $N$
  groups and tests on every combination of $k$ groups, with purging and
  embargo
  - Number of train-test splits: $\binom{N}{k}$
  - Number of complete backtest paths:
    $$
    \varphi[N, k] \triangleq \frac{k}{N} \binom{N}{k}
    $$

- @Example@: $N = 6$ and $k = 2$ give 15 splits and 5 paths

- @Intuition@: each path tests the strategy on history it did not see, e.g.,
  a strategy trained without 2008 is tested on 2008

- @Key idea@: CPCV returns a distribution of Sharpe ratios, and many paths are
  harder to overfit than one

## Deflated Sharpe Ratio

* The Sharpe Ratio Is an Estimate
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:2226 '* Sharpe Ratio'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:2236 '* Probabilistic SR'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:2244 '* Annualized SR'

- @Fact@: $\widehat{SR}$ is estimated from $T$ returns, and its standard error
  grows with negative skew $\gamma_3$ and high kurtosis $\gamma_4$
  $$
  \sigma(\widehat{SR})
    \approx \sqrt{
      \frac{1 - \gamma_3 \widehat{SR} + \frac{\gamma_4 - 1}{4} \widehat{SR}^2}
           {T - 1}
    }
  $$

- @Definition@: the **probabilistic Sharpe ratio** is the probability that
  the true Sharpe ratio exceeds a benchmark $SR^*$
  $$
  PSR(SR^*)
    \triangleq Z\left[
      \frac{(\widehat{SR} - SR^*) \sqrt{T - 1}}
           {\sqrt{1 - \gamma_3 \widehat{SR} + \frac{\gamma_4 - 1}{4}
             \widehat{SR}^2}}
    \right]
  $$

- @Example@: 5 years of monthly returns with annual $SR = 1.0$
  - Gaussian returns: standard error 0.46, $PSR(0) = 0.985$
  - Skew $-2$ and kurtosis 9 (e.g., option selling): standard error 0.60,
    $PSR(0) = 0.953$

- @Remark@: annualizing with $\sqrt{periods}$ assumes IID returns

* Deflated Sharpe Ratio
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:2240 '* Deflated SR'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=55%}
- @Definition@: the **deflated Sharpe ratio** is the PSR against the expected
  maximum Sharpe ratio of the trials
  $$
  DSR \triangleq PSR(SR_0)
  $$
  - $SR_0$ comes from the false strategy theorem with the number of trials
    $N$ and the variance of their Sharpe ratios

- @Example@: 5 years of daily returns, skew $-0.5$, kurtosis 5
  - $SR = 1.0$ loses 95% confidence after 3 trials
  - $SR = 1.5$ after 12 trials
  - $SR = 2.0$ after about 200 trials

- @Key idea@: always report the number of trials together with the Sharpe
  ratio
::::
:::: {.column width=45%}

![](book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/lectures_source/figures/L02.01.Deflated_Sharpe.png)

\footnotesize _Deflated Sharpe ratio of the selected strategy against the number
of trials, with the 95% level dashed_
::::
:::

* Other Corrections: Reality Check and PBO
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.data_mining_bias.txt:41 '## White's reality check'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.data_mining_bias.txt:115 '#### Hansen test for SPA'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1951 '* Probability of Backtest Overfitting (PBO)'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Algorithmic_trading.Chan.2013.txt:553 '* Computing distribution of the null hypothesis'

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Method", "Question it answers", "Procedure"),
  rows: (
    ("Monte Carlo null", "Does the strategy beat random strategies like it?", "Simulate random strategies with the same long-short ratio and holding period"),
    ("White's reality check", "Does the best variant beat a benchmark after the search?", "Bootstrap the maximum performance over all variants ever tested"),
    ("Hansen's SPA test", "Same question, with more power", "Studentize the statistic and limit the effect of poor variants"),
    ("PBO", "How often is the in-sample winner below median out-of-sample?", "Split the trial returns into blocks and test every in-sample and out-of-sample combination"),
  ),
  col-widths: (0.9fr, 1.5fr, 1.8fr),
  bold-first-col: true,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Remark@: **PBO** is the probability of backtest overfitting, estimated by
  combinatorially symmetric cross-validation
  - A PBO near 50% means that the selection process adds no information

- @Key idea@: every method needs the full set of trials, not only the winner

## Why AI

* Experiment Tracking Counts the Trials
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:464 '## 2018, de Prado, A practical solution to the multiple-testing crisis in financial research'
// From: //notes1/notes/work.trading_ideas.txt:346 '## Measuring overfitting'

- @Problem@: DSR, BH, and PBO need the number of trials, which researchers
  rarely record
  - Many trials on the same data make luck look like skill

- @Solution@: experiment tracking logs every trial automatically
  - Strategy configuration, data version, code version, and metrics
  - The log is append-only, so no failed trial can be deleted
// TODO(gp): Check the 6,385 trials: they come from the reporting template
// in the de Prado (2018) multiple-testing paper, not from a live strategy
  - E.g., a proposed reporting template for a corporate-bond strategy
    discloses the 6,385 trials behind it

- @Techniques@: ML turns the trial log into the inputs of the corrections
  - Cluster the trials by the correlation of their returns to estimate the
    number of effectively independent trials
  - Use the variance of Sharpe ratios across clusters in the DSR

- @Remark@: research agents can run thousands of backtests per hour
  - Automated research multiplies $N$, so the trial log must be mandatory

* A Backtest Protocol
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Advances_in_Financial_ML.de_Prado.2018.md:1917 '* Steps to reduce overfitting'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:319 '* Sensitivity analysis'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Quantitative_trading.Chan.2009.txt:346 '* Strategy refinement'

- @Techniques@:
  1. State the hypothesis and its economic mechanism before touching data
  2. Specify the strategy fully, then backtest it
  3. Simulate realistically: point-in-time data, survivorship-free universe,
     next-bar fills, calibrated costs
  4. Log every trial, including the failures
  5. Validate with purged and embargoed CV or CPCV
  6. Report DSR and PBO with the number of trials
  7. Run a sensitivity analysis: small parameter changes should not change
     the result much
  8. Paper trade before committing capital

- @Key idea@: if a backtest fails, start from scratch instead of tweaking the
  model until it passes

- @Remark@: prefer models that work across many instruments and asset
  classes, since many markets are harder to overfit than one security

# Causal vs. Predictive

* Predictive vs. Causal Questions
// From: book_springer/lectures_source/Lesson02.1_From_Data_Science_To_Decision_Science.smd:48 '* Causal vs Predictive Questions'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Causal_Factor_Investing.de_Prado.2023.md:94 '* Data-generating process'

- @Comparison@: each investment question has a predictive form and a causal
  form

\begingroup \scriptsize
```{=typst}
#styled-table(
  headers: ("Predictive question", "Causal question"),
  rows: (
    ("Which stocks will outperform next month?", "Does exposure to value earn a premium, or does it proxy for another variable?"),
    ("Will volatility rise tomorrow?", "How much will my order move the price?"),
    ("Does positive news precede returns?", "Will trading on news still pay once others trade on it?"),
    ("Which loans will default?", "Does a lower interest rate reduce defaults?"),
  ),
  bold-first-col: false,
)
```
\endgroup

- @Definition@: $X$ **causes** $Y$ when _setting_ $X$ changes the distribution
  of $Y$
  $$
  \Pr(Y = y | do(X = x)) \neq \Pr(Y = y)
  $$
  - Setting is different from observing: $\Pr(Y | do(X)) \neq \Pr(Y | X)$
    in general

- @Key idea@: a backtest is a causal question, _"what would the PnL have been
  if I had traded?"_, answered with observational data and a cost model

* Spurious Factors and Confounding
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Causal_Factor_Investing.de_Prado.2023.md:76 '# 2. Association vs Causation'
// From: book_springer/lectures_source/Lesson02.1_From_Data_Science_To_Decision_Science.smd:100 '* Correlation Encodes Confounding as Causation'
// From: book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/paper_list.md:83 '### [ ] 2025, López de Prado et al., "Causality and Factor Investing: A Primer"'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: a **confounder** $Z$ causes both the characteristic $X$ and the
  return $Y$, which creates an association between $X$ and $Y$ with no causal
  link

// TODO(gp): Check the analyst coverage example: it is an illustration not
// taken from a source, so consider a documented confounded factor instead
- @Example@: analyst coverage and returns
  - Large firms attract more analysts, and size is associated with returns
  - Regressing returns on coverage alone credits coverage with part of the
    size effect

- @Key idea@: omitting a confounder biases the factor premium and can flip
  its sign
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz
digraph Confounder {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.6;
    ranksep=0.6;
    rankdir=TB;
    // confounder : violet | characteristic : orange | return : blue
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.8, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=12, margin="0.22,0.12", height=0.45];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    Z [label="Firm size Z", fillcolor="#E9E1F7", color="#A88FD9", fontcolor="#45296B"];
    X [label="Analyst coverage X", fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Y [label="Return Y", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];

    Z -> X;
    Z -> Y;
    X -> Y [style=dashed, color="#D98C8C", arrowhead=none, label="  association only  "];
    { rank=same; X; Y; }
}
```
::::
:::

* Collider Bias and the Factor Mirage
// From: book_springer/lectures_source/Lesson02.1_From_Data_Science_To_Decision_Science.smd:297 '* Berkson's Paradox'
// From: book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/paper_list.md:83 '### [ ] 2025, López de Prado et al., "Causality and Factor Investing: A Primer"'
// From: book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/paper_list.md:73 '### [ ] 2026, López de Prado et al., "Correcting the Factor Mirage: A Research'

::: columns
:::: {.column width=60%}
- @Definition@: a **collider** $C$ is caused by both $X$ and $Y$, and
  conditioning on it creates an association between $X$ and $Y$

// TODO(gp): Check the leverage collider example: it is a constructed
// illustration, not an empirical result from a paper
- @Example@: survivorship bias is collider bias
  - High leverage and low returns both cause delisting
  - Levered survivors must have had high returns, so a spurious leverage
    premium appears

- @Definition@: a **factor mirage** is a factor model that looks
  statistically valid but is causally misspecified
  - It can lose money even when every premium has the correct sign
::::
:::: {.column width=40%}

```graphviz
digraph Collider {
    bgcolor="transparent";
    pad="0.15";
    splines=spline;
    nodesep=0.6;
    ranksep=0.6;
    rankdir=TB;
    // characteristic : orange | return : blue | collider : coral
    node [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", penwidth=1.8, fontname="Helvetica",
          fontsize=12, margin="0.22,0.12", height=0.45];
    edge [color="#A3B1C0", penwidth=1.3, arrowhead=vee, arrowsize=0.75,
          fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, fontcolor="#7B8794"];

    X [label="Leverage X", fillcolor="#FBEBD4", color="#D9A85F", fontcolor="#6B4517"];
    Y [label="Return Y", fillcolor="#D3E3F3", color="#7CA6CE", fontcolor="#1F4E79"];
    C [label="Survival C\n(conditioned on)", fillcolor="#F6C6C6", color="#D98C8C", fontcolor="#6B2A2A"];

    X -> C;
    Y -> C;
    X -> Y [style=dashed, color="#D98C8C", arrowhead=none, label="  spurious  "];
    { rank=same; X; Y; }
}
```
::::
:::

* Falsification and Causal Mechanisms
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Causal_Factor_Investing.de_Prado.2023.md:160 '* The Three Steps of Scientific Discovery'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Causal_Factor_Investing.de_Prado.2023.md:195 '* Probability of informed trading (PIN) theory'
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Causal_Factor_Investing.de_Prado.2023.md:243 '* Falsification Through "Risky Forecasts"'
// From: //notes1/notes/papers.finance.misc.txt:412 '### Problem #2: Finance is an adaptive system'

- @Components@: the three steps of scientific discovery
  1. _Phenomenological_: observe a recurrent association
  2. _Theoretical_: propose a testable causal mechanism
  3. _Falsification_: deduce implications and try to prove them false

- @Example@: the probability of informed trading theory
  - Order flow imbalance $\to$ market maker losses $\to$ wider spread
    $\to$ less liquidity $\to$ higher volatility
  - Each link involves measurable variables and is testable on its own

- @Techniques@: falsify through _risky forecasts_
  - Predict the outcome of an intervention not yet observed, then wait for
    it, as in an out-of-sample test

- @Key idea@: most factor research stops at step 1, and a backtest alone
  cannot go beyond it

- @Remark@: finance is adaptive, so the discovery of an effect can erase the
  effect

## Why AI

* Causal ML for Factors
// From: //notes1/notes/IN_PROGRESS.finance.Causal_Factor_Investing.de_Prado.2023.md:182 '* Theoretical Step'
// From: book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/paper_list.md:197 '### [ ] 2023, Kiciman et al., "Causal Reasoning and Large Language Models:'
// From: book_Modern_AI_for_Finance/paper_list.md:183 '### [ ] 2023, Zecevic et al., "Causal Parrots: Large Language Models May Talk'

- @Problem@: predictive patterns without causal links fail out of sample, and
  the factor zoo hides which links are causal

- @Techniques@:
  - _Causal discovery_: search for graphs consistent with the data and with
    known constraints (e.g., returns cannot cause past characteristics)
  - _Causal effect estimation_: estimate a premium while adjusting for the
    confounders in the graph, with flexible ML models for the adjustment
  - _LLMs_: propose candidate mechanisms and confounders from papers and
    filings

- @Limitations@:
  - Causal discovery needs assumptions that the data cannot test
  - LLMs can repeat causal claims from their training text without causal
    reasoning (_"causal parrots"_)
  - ML cannot create signal or identification that the data do not contain

- @Key idea@: AI speeds up the theoretical step, but falsification still
  needs out-of-sample evidence

* Summary

- @Key idea@: a backtest is a simulation of one historical path, and it can
  reject a strategy but cannot prove it

- @Components@: backtest mechanics
  - Lag every decision, fill at tradable prices, and charge realistic costs
  - Use point-in-time, survivorship-free data, since leaks inflate silently
  - Test the backtester like production code, and paper trade at the end

- @Components@: backtest discipline
  - The best of $N$ zero-skill trials has a high Sharpe ratio by luck
  - Correct for multiple testing with FWER or FDR control and the $t > 3$
    hurdle
  - Validate with purged, embargoed, and combinatorial CV, and report the DSR
    with the number of trials

- @Components@: causal vs. predictive
  - Confounders and colliders create factors that predict in-sample and fail
    out of sample
  - A factor needs a falsifiable mechanism, not only a backtest

